// mp-rs-st-est (racy) -- the non-atomic-data variant, with the coherence order pinned to 2.
//
// rmm-zoo: litmus/cpp_memory_model/rs/ -- the release-sequence family, vendored
//   from gonzalobg/cpp_memory_model and checked in the zoo against the
//   per-version C++ cat models (cpp11.cat / cpp17.cat / cpp2w.cat, herd7 7.58).
// Source: ISO/IEC 14882:2011, :2017, :2020 (the [atomics.order] release-sequence
//   definition); Boehm, Giroux & Vafeiadis, P0668R5 "Revising the C++ Memory
//   Model" (2018); Boehm, P0982R1 "Weaken Release Sequences" (2018).
//
// Reference verdicts (herd7 7.58, from the zoo's rs/README.md):
//   C++11 FORBIDS (racy)  |  C++17 ALLOWS (racy)  |  C++20 ALLOWS (racy)
//
// The only member of the family whose C++11 verdict is Never while C++17 and
// C++20 allow it: under C++11 the relaxed store of 3 is still in the release
// sequence, so an acquire reading 3 synchronises and the data read is not
// stale. From C++17 it is not, and the read races.
//
// Upstream condition: exists([x]=2 /\ 1:a=3 /\ 1:b=0) -- note [x]=2, so P2's
// store is coherence-last even though the reader saw 3.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  y.store(1, na);
  x.store(1, rel);
  x.store(3, rlx)
} ||| {
  r1 := x.load(acq);
  if (r1 = 3) {
    r2 := y.load(na)
  } else {skip}
} ||| {
  x.store(2, rlx)
}
%%
allow (r1 = 3 && r2 = 0) [C17]
